'''''Amaranthus deflexus''''' is also known by the common names '''low amaranth''', '''Argentina amaranth''', '''perennial pigweed''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> and '''large-fruit amaranth'''.<ref>{{Cite book|url=http://www.forest.go.kr/kna/special/download/English_Names_for_Korean_Native_Plants.pdf |title=English Names for Korean Native Plants |publisher=[[Korea National Arboretum]] |year=2015 |isbn=978-89-97450-98-5 |location=Pocheon |pages=349 |access-date=25 January 2016 |via=[[Korea Forest Service]] |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170525105020/http://www.forest.go.kr/kna/special/download/English_Names_for_Korean_Native_Plants.pdf |archivedate=25 May 2017 }}</ref> It is native to [[South America]], and has been introduced to many other parts of the world.<ref name="POWO_327382-2">{{cite web |title=''Amaranthus deflexus'' L. |work=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew|url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:327382-2 |accessdate=2020-07-02 }}</ref> It is a short-lived [[Perennial plant|perennial]] or [[annual plant]].  The plant can grow up to 1.5&nbsp;ft (0.5 m) in height.

It flowers in the summer to fall. It has been introduced into many warm or temperate regions of the globe.  It grows best in weedy areas or in disturbed habitats.

A natural hybrid of ''Amaranthus deflexus'' and ''[[Amaranthus muricatus]]'' has been described in Europe, and is known as ''Amaranthus'' × ''tarraconensis.''

